From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Wang <wy721@qq.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 10:11:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 7/8] sched/smp: batch wake_up_q() notification IPIs in TDX
 guests

Every IPI sent by a TDX guest costs a TD exit and a TD entry. When a
task wakes many sleeping tasks through wake_up_q() (futex wake,
condition variable broadcast, OpenMP thread pools), each wakee on a
halted CPU costs one separate IPI hypercall, and the waker spends most
of its time in TD transitions instead of issuing wakeups.

Add a runtime switch, tdx_wake_q_batch
(/sys/module/kernel/parameters/tdx_wake_q_batch), that keeps publishing
each wakeup immediately but defers the notification: target CPUs are
collected in a stack-local mask and notified with one mask IPI. A group
ends after 8 wakeups, after about 50 microseconds of sender time or when
the sender needs to reschedule, so the first wakee of a group waits at most
for the rest of that small group to be queued. Wakees that are polling in
idle still need no IPI at all.

Batched TTWU entries use a separate per-CPU llist so that a synchronous
call-single callback is never hidden behind a deferred notification. The
IPI handler and the explicit idle, migration and CPU-down flush paths drain
both lists. Only task-context wake_up_q() and fair-class wakeups take the
batched path; RT, deadline, stop and ext tasks, interrupt-context wakeups
and direct try_to_wake_up() callers keep immediate notification. The switch
can only be enabled in a TDX guest without PREEMPT_RT. It is on by default
in TDX guests from a late initcall, so early boot and SMP bring-up keep
immediate notification; an explicit tdx_wake_q_batch= boot parameter is
preserved.

With guest halt polling enabled in a 32-vCPU TDX guest, enabling batching
raised a PyTorch CPU operator with 32 passive OpenMP threads from 257 to
4440 MiB/s and doubled two many-thread pool synchronization benchmarks.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [dstack TDX guest tuning, not submitted]
